// Making a request — **written because a consumer needed it**, and that is the finding.
//
// The rewrite of `packages/http` covered `request.wac`, `response.wac`, `headers.wac` and the server
// faults. It has no client, and nothing noticed until `@/packages/ethrpc` tried to make a call:
// `post` and a fault type for it are the two names a caller of this package most obviously wants,
// and the barrel had neither. Half a package, and the half nobody had asked for.
//
// The shipped `packages/http/src/client.wac` answers `Fetched`, which is `(bool ok, Incoming
// response, string why)` — and its `failed` builds an empty `Incoming` to fill the slot, with a
// comment saying so: *"A `Fetched` that is not `ok` still has to carry *an* `Incoming`."* Five ways
// to fail, one sentence, and an invented response.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";
import { Net } from "std";
import { Incoming, Exchange, Received, Limits, read } from "./incoming.wac";
import { ResponseFault } from "./fault.wac";
import { Draft, OutgoingFault, write as writeRequest } from "./outgoing.wac";
import { Request, Method, Post } from "./request.wac";

/**
 * The connection did not open.
 *
 * **The `string why` this carried is gone, because there is nothing to put in it.** The doc said
 * *"the host would not resolve, or the socket would not open"* — two causes — and `Net.connect`
 * answers `Result<Socket, NotGranted>`, so the only thing the layer below can report is *the grant
 * was refused*. The field promised a distinction its source cannot make.
 *
 * Found auditing this directory against its own findings, and it is the inverse of all of them: five
 * days spent making faults more precise than a `bool`, and this one was more precise than the data.
 *
 * The missing distinction is real and belongs to `std` — see the note at the foot.
 */
export struct NoConnection { string host; }

/** The request went out short, or not at all. */
export struct SendFailed { string host; }

/** The connection failed part way through the response — *not* a short body. */
export struct ReadFailed { string host; string why; }

/** The response ended mid-message. */
export struct Incomplete {}

/** Bytes arrived and are not an HTTP response. */
export struct Unparseable { i32 at; }

/**
 * Anything a request can do instead of answering.
 *
 * The five the shipped client distinguishes internally and then flattens into one `string why`. Two
 * of them are worth a caller's attention separately and the sentence does not admit it:
 * `NoConnection` is *try another endpoint*, and `Unparseable` is *this endpoint is not speaking
 * HTTP*, which is a configuration mistake rather than an outage.
 */
export union<NoConnection, SendFailed, ReadFailed, Incomplete, Unparseable> HttpFault;

/**
 * Everything a call can fail with — **three layers' unions, and the shipped one flattens two.**
 *
 * A client does three things and each has its own vocabulary:
 *
 *   * **build** — `./outgoing.wac`'s `OutgoingFault`: the caller set a header the writer owns, or
 *     gave no `Host`.
 *   * **carry** — the five above, minus the one that is not transport: `NoConnection`,
 *     `SendFailed`, `ReadFailed`.
 *   * **read** — `./incoming.wac`'s `ResponseFault`, which is `union<RequestFault, BadStatus>` and
 *     has eleven members.
 *
 * `HttpFault` collapsed the first into nothing and the third into `Unparseable { i32 at; }` — one
 * member with an offset, standing for eleven named ones next door. Nesting is what the lowering
 * supports and what `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac` showed a caller wants, so the three stay three
 * and `Err(is Transport):` is one arm for the middle.
 */
export union<NoConnection, SendFailed, ReadFailed> Transport;
export union<OutgoingFault, Transport, ResponseFault> CallFault;

/**
 * A POST, and the exchange — or which of the three layers refused.
 *
 * **Answers an `Exchange`, not an `Incoming`**, which closes the loop `./incoming.wac` opened: *"a
 * client's outbound request is built by `./client.wac` as bytes and never exists as a value."* It
 * exists here, because `read` needs it and because `Draft.seal` is what makes one.
 */
export async Result<Exchange, CallFault> post(Net net, string url, Bytes body) { … }

/** A GET, the same way. */
export async Result<Exchange, CallFault> get(Net net, string url) { … }

/**
 * The shape all three layers meet in, written out because it is the composition test.
 *
 *     Draft d = Draft.to(Post, target, host);
 *     d.set("Content-Type", "application/json");
 *     Request req = try d.seal();                       // OutgoingFault
 *     Socket s = try await net.connect(host, port);     // Transport
 *     _ = await s.send(writeRequest(req, false));
 *     …
 *     match (read(got, req, eof, limits)) {             // ResponseFault
 *       Ok(x):         { return Result.Ok(x); }
 *       Bad(why):      { return Result.Err(why); }
 *       Incomplete:    { }                              // read more and ask again
 *     }
 *
 * Three `try`s against three different unions, all propagating into `CallFault`, which is the first
 * function in this directory where that has been written down rather than argued about. §2.
 */
export async Result<Exchange, CallFault> call(Net net, const Draft d, string host, i32 port) { … }

// ## A fault cannot be more precise than the capability under it
//
// `NoConnection` carried a `string why` until an audit asked where the string came from.
// `Net.connect` is
//
//     fn<Ticket<Result<Socket, NotGranted>>(string, i32)>  connect;
//
// so **`NotGranted` is the only thing it can say**, and a client cannot tell *the name did not
// resolve* from *the socket was refused* from *this program may not use the network*. Three causes,
// one answer, and the fault type above invented a field to hold a distinction that never arrives.
//
// `ReadFailed`'s `why` is not the same and stays: `Socket.recv` answers a `Read` sum whose `Failed`
// arm carries a `string`, so there is a real message from the host to put in it.
//
// **So this is a `std` finding wearing a package's clothes.** `Net.connect` collapsing a DNS failure,
// a refused connection and a missing grant into one `NotGranted` is the same shape as everything
// this directory has been unpicking — and it is worse than a package's, because a package can be
// rewritten and a capability's answer is what the host gives. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has the
// `NotGranted`-for-everything entry; this is a caller that wanted the distinction and could not get
// one.
//
// And the lesson for the fault types here, which is new: **a fault union is bounded above by what
// its sources can distinguish.** Five members is right only if five things are separately knowable;
// `NoConnection` was a sixth distinction inside one of them, invented at the layer that wanted it
// rather than the layer that could see it.

// ## 2. Three `try`s, three unions, and `try` cannot map any of them
//
// `call` above propagates an `OutgoingFault` from `seal`, a `NotGranted` from `connect` and a
// `ResponseFault` from `read`, into one `CallFault`. Under nominal membership — which
// `@/packages/wacc/src/check.wac` settles is the rule — each `try` is legal exactly when the callee's
// union is a **declared member** of `CallFault`. Two of the three are:
//
//     union<OutgoingFault, Transport, ResponseFault> CallFault;
//
// and the third is not. `net.connect` answers `Result<Socket, NotGranted>`, and `NotGranted` is a
// member of `Transport`'s members rather than of `Transport` — `NoConnection` *contains* the idea and
// is not it. So `try await net.connect(…)` does not typecheck against `CallFault`, and the
// `NoConnection { string host; }` above is what a hand-written `match` builds instead: the fault this
// layer wants, from the one the layer below gives, with the host added because the caller knows it
// and the capability does not.
//
// That is `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *`try` cannot map, so wrapping a callee's fault costs a `match`*
// — and this is the first file where the wrap is **load-bearing rather than tidy**. It is not
// renaming a fault; it is the only place the host's name gets attached to the failure, because
// `Net.connect`'s answer does not carry one.
//
// So of the three layers, two compose by declaration and one needs eight lines. Which is a better
// answer than *`try` needs a mapping form*: the mapping that is wanted here **adds data**, and no
// `try e catch F` spelling would supply the host either.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`read` answers three arms and `call` must loop, so the retry lives in the caller.** `Received`
// is `Ok | Bad | Incomplete` and `Incomplete` means *read more*, so `call` holds a growing buffer and
// asks again. The loop is four lines and the thing it cannot say is **when to stop**: a peer that
// sends nothing keeps the loop honest only because `recv` eventually answers `Failed`, and nothing in
// the types says a client should bound the wait. `@/packages/wactest/src/within.wac` is the timeout
// this wants and it is a test helper.
//
// **`Exchange` makes the request outlive the call, and that is the point and the cost.** A caller
// gets back what it sent, so `x.sent.headers` is readable after the response — which is what makes
// an `Exchange` worth having and also means a client holding a hundred exchanges holds a hundred
// request bodies. The shipped `Fetched` could not do this and did not have to decide; nothing here
// says a caller may drop the half it does not want.
